<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/foundational/memory">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Memory is not a recording; it is three systems in series. The <strong>sensory store</strong>
    holds raw impressions for under a second. <strong>Working memory</strong> keeps a handful of
    items alive for 15–30 seconds — famously "7 ± 2" chunks (George Miller, 1956). What gets
    rehearsed and connected can move into <strong>long-term memory</strong>, whose capacity is,
    as far as anyone can measure, effectively unlimited. The classic multi-store model is
    Atkinson and Shiffrin's (1968); working memory's messier reality was mapped by Baddeley and
    Hitch (1974).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Ebbinghaus's forgetting curve — raise the strength slider (more review, better sleep) and watch decay flatten" widget="plotter" config={forgetCfg} />

  <p>
    Hermann Ebbinghaus spent years memorising nonsense syllables and measured his own retention:
    loss is steep in the first hours and days, then flattens into a long tail (1885). The decay
    constant <code>s</code> is what every study technique is secretly fighting for.
  </p>

  <h2>What actually moves s</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spacing beats massing.</strong> Three one-hour sessions across a week beat three hours in a row — the spacing effect, one of psychology's most replicated findings.</li>
    <li><strong>Testing is studying.</strong> Actively retrieving (flashcards, closing the book and recalling) strengthens memory far more than re-reading, which only builds familiarity.</li>
    <li><strong>Chunking extends working memory.</strong> Expert chess players remember whole boards, not 64 squares — chunks pack more into 7 slots (Chase & Simon, 1973).</li>
    <li><strong>Sleep is part of studying.</strong> Memory consolidation — the move into long-term store — happens largely during sleep; all-nighters mostly delete the day's work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    And a caution: remembering is reconstructive. Each recall rewrites the trace a little, which
    is why confident eyewitnesses can still be wrong.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Ebbinghaus (1885); Miller (1956); Atkinson & Shiffrin (1968); Baddeley & Hitch (1974); Chase & Simon (1973).</p>
</TopicPage>
